270
00:19:22,019 --> 00:19:24,429
I was being modest.
- I don't need modest.

271
00:19:24,430 --> 00:19:26,092
I need people
who think they're right

272
00:19:26,093 --> 00:19:27,600
when everyone else
thinks they're wrong.

273
00:19:27,601 --> 00:19:29,098
I need people
who piss other people off.

274
00:19:29,099 --> 00:19:30,243
That's me!

275
00:19:30,244 --> 00:19:32,104
Do you want to be on a project?

276
00:19:32,105 --> 00:19:37,356
If you think I can help, then

277
00:19:37,357 --> 00:19:39,426
Just answer the question.

278
00:19:39,427 --> 00:19:41,364
I wanna help the world.

279
00:19:41,365 --> 00:19:42,928
I left you three dots.

280
00:19:42,929 --> 00:19:44,436
Just three, huh?

281
00:19:44,437 --> 00:19:45,801
Plus the one you killed.

282
00:19:46,805 --> 00:19:48,344
I would take the three.

283
00:19:50,713 --> 00:19:52,320
Yes?
- I'll take the dots.

284
00:19:52,321 --> 00:19:53,850
Get to work, Dr. Grace.

285
00:19:55,525 --> 00:19:57,891
The world is counting on you.

286
00:21:05,632 --> 00:21:06,963
<i>Hey, Carl?</i>

287
00:21:08,165 --> 00:21:09,507
This is Carl.

288
00:21:09,508 --> 00:21:11,302
Imagine for a second

289
00:21:11,303 --> 00:21:13,713
that you're
an interstellar microorganism.

290
00:21:13,714 --> 00:21:15,485
I'm not doing that.

291
00:21:17,249 --> 00:21:20,188
Why would you
leave the sun and go to Venus?

292
00:21:20,189 --> 00:21:22,863
Why not stop
at Mercury? It's closer.

293
00:21:24,373 --> 00:21:26,332
Maybe fresh air?

294
00:21:26,333 --> 00:21:28,270
Venus's atmosphere
is mostly CO2.

295
00:21:28,271 --> 00:21:29,977
Maybe that's fresh air to them.

296
00:21:29,978 --> 00:21:31,848
You don't know what
these little space dots like.

297
00:21:37,124 --> 00:21:38,323
<i>Hey, Carl?</i>

298
00:21:38,324 --> 00:21:40,228
Do we have an expense account?

299
00:21:40,229 --> 00:21:41,967
We don't.

300
00:21:46,241 --> 00:21:47,351
But I do.

301
00:22:18,878 --> 00:22:20,450
All right,
I got something for you.

302
00:22:31,199 --> 00:22:32,740
Thank you.

303
00:22:32,741 --> 00:22:34,369
Do you have a coupon?
- We don't need a coupon.

304
00:22:34,370 --> 00:22:36,373
We're the government.
- Which government?

305
00:22:36,374 --> 00:22:37,715
All of 'em.

306
00:22:39,589 --> 00:22:41,218
So this box is Venus.

307
00:22:41,219 --> 00:22:43,057
The box is Venus.

308
00:22:43,058 --> 00:22:45,094
It's set to emit the same
infrared light frequency

309
00:22:45,095 --> 00:22:47,032
as Venus's CO2 atmosphere.

310
00:22:47,033 --> 00:22:49,267
So if the dots move
when you turn the lights on,

311
00:22:49,268 --> 00:22:51,943
that means
they're looking for CO2.

312
00:22:51,944 --> 00:22:54,442
Thus proving
the Carl Hypothesis.

313
00:22:54,443 --> 00:22:55,476
Sick.

314
00:22:57,956 --> 00:22:59,452
Sorry.

315
00:22:59,453 --> 00:23:01,159
All right.
- All right.

316
00:23:01,160 --> 00:23:02,799
You ready?

317
00:23:05,641 --> 00:23:07,534
Uh-oh.

318
00:23:07,535 --> 00:23:09,076
Why you saying "Uh-oh"?

319
00:23:09,077 --> 00:23:11,113
The Astrophage
moved off the slide.

320
00:23:11,114 --> 00:23:12,412
Huh?

321
00:23:12,413 --> 00:23:14,053
We lost them.

322
00:23:14,054 --> 00:23:15,990
What do you mean,
we lost them? Go get them.

323
00:23:16,559 --> 00:23:18,391
If I open the door
in the light,

324
00:23:18,392 --> 00:23:20,428
they're gonna escape, and then
we'll lose them forever.

325
00:23:20,429 --> 00:23:21,805
At least now
they're in the box.

326
00:23:21,806 --> 00:23:23,676
So we'll just
turn the lights off.

327
00:23:23,677 --> 00:23:25,273
But there'll still be light.
It's gotta be total darkness.

328
00:23:25,274 --> 00:23:27,551
Okay, so just put this box
in another box.

329
00:23:36,197 --> 00:23:37,362
Nice.

330
00:23:49,520 --> 00:23:51,765
Go ahead and close it. Over.

331
00:23:52,460 --> 00:23:53,967
I can hear you.

332
00:23:59,513 --> 00:24:01,509
Entering phase two.

333
00:24:07,094 --> 00:24:08,424
I'm in.

334
00:24:13,601 --> 00:24:15,471
<i>Please report. Over.</i>

335
00:24:18,578 --> 00:24:20,085
<i>There's one.</i>

336
00:24:21,650 --> 00:24:22,728
<i>There's two!</i>

337
00:24:22,729 --> 00:24:24,831
Come on! Get the third one.

338
00:24:27,002 --> 00:24:28,663
<i>Got sample three.</i>

339
00:24:28,664 --> 00:24:30,744
<i>The Three Musketeers.
The Three Stooges.</i>

340
00:24:34,544 --> 00:24:35,854
<i>Uh-oh.</i>

341
00:24:35,855 --> 00:24:37,515
What? What?

342
00:24:38,717 --> 00:24:40,489
<i>Are you sitting down?</i>

343
00:24:40,490 --> 00:24:42,328
No, I'm standing
like a grown man.

344
00:24:42,329 --> 00:24:44,397
<i>'Cause there's a fourth.</i>

345
00:24:45,269 --> 00:24:46,302
What?

346
00:24:48,440 --> 00:24:50,673
<i>We're fathers, Carl.</i>

347
00:24:53,054 --> 00:24:55,089
Now I'm sitting down.

348
00:24:57,998 --> 00:24:59,065
<i>Stratt.</i>

349
00:24:59,066 --> 00:25:02,104
Carl and I made a baby.

350
00:25:02,105 --> 00:25:03,535
<i>What?</i>

351
00:25:03,536 --> 00:25:05,439
We figured out
how Astrophage breeds.

352
00:25:06,707 --> 00:25:07,983
<i>Don't move.</i>

353
00:25:09,956 --> 00:25:11,253
Wheels up.

354
00:25:12,653 --> 00:25:14,128
- You know, Carl,

355
00:25:14,129 --> 00:25:16,528
when Stratt said
she was sending a jet,

356
00:25:16,529 --> 00:25:18,169
I thought you meant
like a PJ or something.

357
00:25:18,170 --> 00:25:20,877
I know.
- Take this.

358
00:25:22,343 --> 00:25:23,884
Been a long time
since I've taken a pill

359
00:25:23,885 --> 00:25:25,479
from a stranger
without knowing what it was.

360
00:25:27,716 --> 00:25:29,829
Stop! Stop!

361
00:25:40,247 --> 00:25:42,117
Dr. Grace,
how was your flight?

362
00:25:47,690 --> 00:25:49,396
Where are we?
- The ocean.

363
00:25:49,397 --> 00:25:52,468
The Astrophage reproduction.
Can you do it at scale?

364
00:25:52,469 --> 00:25:56,377
Oh, in theory, you just get
a bunch of elbow-shaped pipe,

365
00:25:56,378 --> 00:25:57,842
put sunlight in one end--

366
00:25:57,843 --> 00:25:59,052
Excuse me for a second.

367
00:26:00,419 --> 00:26:01,959
Write it down.
- Yes, ma'am.

368
00:26:03,557 --> 00:26:06,463
and IR light and CO2
in the other. Repeat.

369
00:26:06,464 --> 00:26:07,663
Anything else you need for--

370
00:26:07,664 --> 00:26:09,304
Just some water.

371
00:26:09,305 --> 00:26:10,636
He gave me a pill
and it's still in my throat.

372
00:26:10,637 --> 00:26:12,046
Two venti coffees.

373
00:26:12,047 --> 00:26:13,543
Thank you so much.
Water for him.

374
00:26:13,544 --> 00:26:14,777
Or coffee. That's

375
00:26:14,778 --> 00:26:16,219
I need both.

376
00:26:16,220 --> 00:26:17,683
Or not.

377
00:26:17,684 --> 00:26:19,060
Okay, so this is
kind of a tough room,

378
00:26:19,061 --> 00:26:20,689
so don't take it personally.

379
00:26:20,690 --> 00:26:22,231
I don't need anything fancy.

380
00:26:22,232 --> 00:26:24,498
As long as there's a bed
and a shower I'm good.

381
00:26:29,444 --> 00:26:31,414
I can't do this.
- Come on. Yes.

382
00:26:31,415 --> 00:26:33,286
I can't. I can't.
- You're doing great.

383
00:26:33,287 --> 00:26:35,587
Everyone,
this is Dr. Ryland Grace

384
00:26:35,588 --> 00:26:36,953
from the United States.

385
00:26:36,954 --> 00:26:39,265
Please go here. Thank you.

386
00:26:39,266 --> 00:26:42,138
And he figured out
how to breed Astrophage.

387
00:26:47,645 --> 00:26:49,219
How did you do it?

388
00:26:49,220 --> 00:26:50,716
How long
does the process take?

389
00:26:50,717 --> 00:26:53,227
Does it reproduce
by mitosis or meiosis?

390
00:26:53,228 --> 00:26:55,527
<i>What is the incubation period?</i>

391
00:26:56,763 --> 00:26:58,401
Um

392
00:26:59,835 --> 00:27:02,343
Carl and I made a mini Venus
out of a plywood box,

393
00:27:02,946 --> 00:27:05,416
and as soon as
the samples recognized

394
00:27:05,417 --> 00:27:08,454
the, uh, spectral signature
of CO2, they were

395
00:27:10,724 --> 00:27:12,628
They were like,
"Whoomp! There it is!"

396
00:27:12,629 --> 00:27:13,796
Okay.

397
00:27:13,797 --> 00:27:15,370
You can sit down.

398
00:27:15,371 --> 00:27:16,934
Who's Carl?

399
00:27:16,935 --> 00:27:18,508
While your experiment
was crude and clumsy,

400
00:27:18,509 --> 00:27:20,974
our scientists
have replicated his results.

401
00:27:23,751 --> 00:27:25,720
I'm sorry, how did you see--?

402
00:27:25,721 --> 00:27:27,592
We estimate
an eight-day doubling time

403
00:27:27,593 --> 00:27:28,925
under optimal conditions.

404
00:27:28,926 --> 00:27:31,237
Dr. Grace is right.
- Oh.

405
00:27:31,238 --> 00:27:32,734
It is the CO2.

406
00:27:32,735 --> 00:27:34,309
That's why
they're going to Venus.

407
00:27:34,310 --> 00:27:35,608
Please say it for everyone.

408
00:27:35,609 --> 00:27:37,216
That's why
they're going to Venus.

409
00:27:37,217 --> 00:27:40,321
So that's why
they're going to Venus,

410
00:27:40,322 --> 00:27:42,193
is what Dr. Grace wants to say.

411
00:27:42,194 --> 00:27:43,723
They're going to breed.

412
00:27:43,724 --> 00:27:45,627
Whoomp! There it is.

413
00:27:47,204 --> 00:27:49,064
I really like him.

414
00:27:49,065 --> 00:27:51,607
How long
to make two million kilogram?

415
00:27:51,608 --> 00:27:53,115
Two million?

416
00:27:54,978 --> 00:27:56,254
Two million is--

417
00:27:56,255 --> 00:27:57,321
Why

418
00:27:59,118 --> 00:28:01,528
on earth would you need
that much Astrophage?

419
00:28:01,529 --> 00:28:03,630
Nobody told him?

420
00:28:04,304 --> 00:28:05,536
He has no clearance.

421
00:28:05,537 --> 00:28:07,374
Please stand up, Dr. Grace.

422
00:28:08,136 --> 00:28:09,246
Stand up.

423
00:28:10,316 --> 00:28:12,143
I hereby grant you
top-secret clearance

424
00:28:12,144 --> 00:28:14,652
to all information pertaining
to Project Hail Mary.

425
00:28:15,921 --> 00:28:17,526
What's Project Hail Mary?

426
00:28:19,400 --> 00:28:20,433
All right.

427
00:28:23,805 --> 00:28:26,347
The sun
is not the only star dying.

428
00:28:26,348 --> 00:28:28,748
There is a clear pattern
of infection.

429
00:28:28,749 --> 00:28:32,689
Every star was infected
by its neighbor except one.

430
00:28:32,690 --> 00:28:34,121
Tau Ceti?

431
00:28:34,122 --> 00:28:35,861
That's right.
11.9 light years away.

432
00:28:35,862 --> 00:28:37,699
It is uninfected

433
00:28:37,700 --> 00:28:39,703
despite being well within
the cluster of infected stars.

434
00:28:39,704 --> 00:28:41,906
Why? Tell him.

435
00:28:41,907 --> 00:28:43,612
We don't know!

436
00:28:43,613 --> 00:28:45,914
Which is why
we decided to build a ship

437
00:28:45,915 --> 00:28:47,686
to go there and find out.

438
00:28:47,687 --> 00:28:49,261
It's 11.9 light years away.

439
00:28:49,262 --> 00:28:51,628
You can't just
build an interstellar ship.

440
00:28:51,629 --> 00:28:52,994
Oh yeah, we can.

441
00:28:52,995 --> 00:28:54,667
The ship
is not really the problem.

442
00:28:54,668 --> 00:28:58,103
The energy required to power
the ship is the problem.

443
00:28:58,104 --> 00:28:59,214
It was the problem.

444
00:29:01,715 --> 00:29:03,454
The Astrophage is the fuel.

445
00:29:03,455 --> 00:29:05,249
Provided we can
make enough of it.

446
00:29:05,250 --> 00:29:07,891
And for that,
we need you, my friend.

447
00:29:10,227 --> 00:29:11,834
Me?

448
00:29:11,835 --> 00:29:14,475
Ooh. Those little guys
store a lot of energy.

449
00:29:15,078 --> 00:29:16,711
One mistake
and that much Astrophage

450
00:29:16,712 --> 00:29:18,914
could vaporize California.

451
00:29:18,915 --> 00:29:21,149
That's true, which is why
you now live on a boat

452
00:29:21,150 --> 00:29:22,921
in the middle
of the ocean in case

453
00:29:24,464 --> 00:29:26,632
I live on a boat?
- You do.

454
00:29:26,633 --> 00:29:29,198
So you wanna build
a near-light speed spaceship,

455
00:29:29,199 --> 00:29:31,543
have it travel further
than any human-made object

456
00:29:31,544 --> 00:29:32,876
has ever traveled,

457
00:29:32,877 --> 00:29:36,652
and visit a star
just to "see what's up"?

458
00:29:36,653 --> 00:29:37,853
Yeah.

459
00:29:37,854 --> 00:29:39,526
And then what?

460
00:29:39,527 --> 00:29:41,365
There won't be
enough fuel for a round-trip

461
00:29:41,366 --> 00:29:44,535
so they send their findings
back to Earth on probes.

462
00:29:46,905 --> 00:29:49,611
And the astronauts

463
00:29:52,818 --> 00:29:54,490
die in space?

464
00:29:54,491 --> 00:29:55,689
Yes.

465
00:29:57,795 --> 00:29:59,400
Space.

466
00:30:02,838 --> 00:30:05,578
Any other plans
you've thought of?

467
00:30:05,579 --> 00:30:08,452
Anything else
you're kicking around?

468
00:30:08,453 --> 00:30:10,754
There are
infinite possibilities

469
00:30:10,755 --> 00:30:12,460
for this to go wrong.

470
00:30:12,461 --> 00:30:13,958
It almost certainly won't work.

471
00:30:13,959 --> 00:30:16,324
It's what you Americans
would call a long shot.

472
00:30:17,064 --> 00:30:18,438
Hail Mary.

473
00:30:18,903 --> 00:30:20,267
I get it now.

474
00:30:20,268 --> 00:30:23,878
The alternative
is to just do nothing

475
00:30:24,680 --> 00:30:26,885
and to starve
and to kill each other

476
00:30:26,886 --> 00:30:29,318
and watch everything
on this planet go extinct,

477
00:30:29,319 --> 00:30:30,594
including us.

478
00:30:31,224 --> 00:30:32,323
So

479
00:30:52,773 --> 00:30:55,337
That's a long time
to be in space.

480
00:30:55,338 --> 00:30:57,010
They'll be in comas
for most of it.

481
00:30:57,545 --> 00:30:59,345
But is that even safe?

482
00:30:59,346 --> 00:31:01,348
Nothing about this is safe.

483
00:31:16,248 --> 00:31:17,589
<i>We only need three.</i>

484
00:31:18,516 --> 00:31:19,726
<i>A pilot.</i>

485
00:31:19,727 --> 00:31:21,830
<i>An engineer.</i>

486
00:31:21,831 --> 00:31:24,031
<i>And a scientist.</i>

487
00:31:28,845 --> 00:31:30,406
Commander Yáo.

488
00:31:32,754 --> 00:31:35,284
I must have known you,
but I just don't remember.

489
00:31:36,255 --> 00:31:38,831
You make a funny face

490
00:31:38,832 --> 00:31:42,463
in literally every picture.

491
00:31:43,500 --> 00:31:46,472
<i>You must have been very smart</i>

492
00:31:46,473 --> 00:31:47,506
<i>and strong</i>

493
00:31:49,347 --> 00:31:50,721
and brave.

494
00:31:54,831 --> 00:31:55,864
Ilyukhina.

495
00:31:57,363 --> 00:31:59,839
First, I owe you
three bags of vodka.

496
00:32:00,975 --> 00:32:03,208
<i>You seemed
to have had a lot of friends.</i>

497
00:32:03,882 --> 00:32:05,312
<i>This picture of you,</i>

498
00:32:05,313 --> 00:32:08,625
what looks like
sneaking into the Kremlin

499
00:32:10,356 --> 00:32:11,422
is legendary.

500
00:32:14,298 --> 00:32:16,135
<i>I wish you were still here.</i>

501
00:32:17,535 --> 00:32:19,814
<i>I wish I wasn't alone.</i>

502
00:32:19,815 --> 00:32:21,574
I wish I was

503
00:32:22,688 --> 00:32:24,922
doing a better job, I just, uh

504
00:32:29,802 --> 00:32:31,704
You were both very loved.

505
00:32:33,633 --> 00:32:34,777
You deserve
much more than this.

506
00:32:37,608 --> 00:32:39,182
I'll do my best

507
00:32:39,183 --> 00:32:40,756
to make sure that you don't

508
00:32:43,488 --> 00:32:45,490
you know, that you didn't--

509
00:32:57,417 --> 00:32:59,056
I'll do my best.

510
00:34:04,584 --> 00:34:06,819
<i>Approaching Tau Ceti orbit.</i>

511
00:34:06,820 --> 00:34:08,161
<i>Prepare for engine cutoff.</i>

512
00:34:09,793 --> 00:34:11,366
<i>- Fourteen, thirteen</i>
- What?

513
00:34:11,367 --> 00:34:12,908
Why engine cutoff?

514
00:34:12,909 --> 00:34:15,572
Seems like something
we should talk about.

515
00:34:15,573 --> 00:34:17,345
<i>Pilot detected.</i>

516
00:34:17,346 --> 00:34:19,547
- What? No. I'm not a pilot!

517
00:34:19,548 --> 00:34:21,056
<i>Four, three</i>

518
00:34:21,057 --> 00:34:22,993
What happens at zero? I mean--

519
00:34:30,868 --> 00:34:33,277
<i>You are now orbiting Tau Ceti.</i>

520
00:34:36,219 --> 00:34:39,994
What the fudging fudger?

521
00:34:42,726 --> 00:34:47,107
<i>Petrovascope operational.</i>

522
00:35:08,316 --> 00:35:09,415
It's Tau Ceti.

523
00:35:20,406 --> 00:35:22,905
You have a Petrova line.

524
00:35:22,906 --> 00:35:24,810
But you're not dimming.

525
00:35:24,811 --> 00:35:26,747
Why?

526
00:35:36,735 --> 00:35:38,176
What is that?

527
00:35:55,774 --> 00:35:57,544
<i>Blip-A detected.</i>

528
00:35:58,482 --> 00:35:59,945
What's a Blip-A?

529
00:36:00,817 --> 00:36:03,061
<i>Blip-A detected.</i>

530
00:37:08,017 --> 00:37:11,429
No, no. No, no, no.
Let's go. Let's go, Mary.

531
00:37:11,430 --> 00:37:13,059
No-No-No-No!

532
00:37:13,060 --> 00:37:14,303
Pilot detected!

533
00:37:14,304 --> 00:37:15,734
<i>Please engage restraint.</i>

534
00:37:15,735 --> 00:37:17,441
Pilot detected!

